      "handed_out": true,
      "brief": "This is a sprint, not a term project: in a short, compressed window you will pick a small\ndata problem, build something that actually runs, and show it off.\n\n## Task\n\nChoose a dataset and a question you can make real progress on quickly - better a small,\nworking end-to-end pipeline than an ambitious one that only half-runs by the deadline.\nSpend the first stretch of time getting something crude working front to back before you\npolish any single part of it; a rough baseline that produces an answer beats a\nbeautifully engineered feature pipeline with nothing at the end of it.\n\nBecause this repository is public from the day it is handed out, treat every commit as\nsomething a stranger could read. Keep the write-up short and honest about what worked and\nwhat did not; a hackathon project that admits its limitations is more convincing than one\nthat oversells a result nobody can reproduce.\n\nSuggested approach:\n\n- Pick a problem you can scope down to something finishable.\n- Get a crude end-to-end version working before improving any one part.\n- Keep a short running log of decisions and dead ends as you go.\n- Prepare a brief demo: what it does, what it does not, and why.\n\n## What to submit\n\nPush your notebook or code, and a short write-up, to `main`. This repository is public\nfrom day one, so commit nothing private or sensitive - no credentials, no personal data,\nnothing you would not want indexed.\n\n## Grading\n\n- Prototype (40%): does it run end to end, and does it answer the question you set yourself?\n- Demo (30%): can you explain what you built and why, clearly and honestly?\n- Write-up (30%): a short, accurate account of the approach, its limits and what you would\n  do with more time.",
